# #855 (a #850 follow-up, 2026-09-19) -- Cloudflare Workers' own default
# for every static asset is `Cache-Control: public, max-age=0,
# must-revalidate` (confirmed against https://developers.cloudflare.com/
# workers/static-assets/headers/, not assumed), which forces a real
# network round-trip for every asset on every load -- a fast 304 on a
# hit, but still a full round-trip's worth of latency, paid again and
# again. Measured directly (real GPRS-equivalent throttling, Playwright's
# CDP Network.emulateNetworkConditions, against the real production
# build): a normal reload with a warm cache took ~12.5s -- indistinguishable
# from having the browser's HTTP cache disabled entirely -- proof that
# must-revalidate's round-trip, not bandwidth, is the actual cost here.
# ~9.4s (a real ~25% improvement) with this file in place.
#
# vite.deploy.config.js's own entryFileNames/chunkFileNames give every
# chunk under /logbook/chunks/ a content hash in its filename -- any
# code change produces a new URL, so a cached copy of the OLD URL can
# never go stale; safe to cache "forever" (no revalidation at all, not
# even a fast one). The top-level *-app.js/tailwind.css/sw.js files
# deliberately keep the platform default: they have stable, un-hashed
# names (the HTML references them by a fixed URL), so an edit to one
# needs the browser to actually notice via revalidation -- immutable
# caching there would mean a real deploy silently never reaching an
# already-visited browser.
/logbook/chunks/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31556952, immutable
